    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It seems the originalist response essentially turns the \"suicide pact\" argument on its head—suggesting that it is actually the *flexible* interpretation that turns the Constitution into a suicide pact by making rights vulnerable to the \"shifting tides of public opinion.\"\n\nGiven that, how would a scholar evaluate the legitimacy of the contemporary \"safety-based\" arguments? If those arguments are based on the idea that the text must account for modern mass casualty events, would an originalist view those arguments as a legitimate part of the legal conversation, or would they characterize them as an attempt to bypass the actual text of the Amendment entirely? I'm interested in whether they see these modern interpretations as being even remotely grounded in actual legal doctrine.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
